function handleDragEnd(event: DragEndEvent) {
		const { active, over } = event;

		if (over && active.id !== over.id) {
			const oldIndex = localKeyedValues.findIndex(
				(item) => item.key === active.id,
			);
			const newIndex = localKeyedValues.findIndex(
				(item) => item.key === over.id,
			);

			if (oldIndex !== -1 && newIndex !== -1) {
				moveItem(newIndex, oldIndex);
			}
		}
	}

findings.body:
`handleDragEnd` computes `oldIndex` (the dragged row) and `newIndex` (the row it was dropped on), then calls `moveItem(newIndex, oldIndex)`. `moveItem(from, to)` removes the item at `from` and inserts it at `to`, so the arguments are backwards.

With `[A, B, C]`, dragging A onto C gives `oldIndex = 0` and `newIndex = 2`. The call becomes `moveItem(2, 0)`, which removes C and puts it at the top: `[C, A, B]`. The intended result is `[B, C, A]`. Every pointer or keyboard reorder is wrong, and the menu's Move up and Move down still work, so the defect only shows up when dragging.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="130-145"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The snapshot tests can't catch this because they never fire a drag event. A test that dispatches a drag end, or that calls `handleDragEnd` through the DndContext, would have failed here.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="142-142"}
moveItem(oldIndex, newIndex);

`prefixItemsCount` is computed at lines 45-47 but never read. `tsconfig.app.json` sets `noUnusedLocals: true` and `biome.json` sets `noUnusedVariables: "error"`, so `tsc` and the lint step will both fail on this file.

The prefix check is already done by `getCanMoveForIndex` and `getPropertyForIndex`, so the variable can simply be removed.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="45-47"}

The PR description lists behavioral tests for the Move up/Move down menu options and a test that prefix items cannot be moved. The diff to `schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx` adds only four snapshot tests under `describe("array")`. None of them click the menu, fire a drag, or assert on the resulting values, so the reorder bug above passes the suite.

Snapshots of the DOM show that a handle is present or absent, but they don't show that the row order changes when an action runs. Bad tests give false confidence here: the suite looks green while the feature is broken.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx" lines="489-565"}

The comment at line 147 says the sortable keys are for "items that can be dragged (non-prefix items)", but `sortableKeys` at line 148 maps over every row, including prefix rows. Prefix rows opt out of dragging through `useSortable({ disabled: !canMove })` in the item component, but the list-level code doesn't filter them, and `handleDragEnd` doesn't check `getCanMoveForIndex` for either the active or the over row.

Whether a prefix row can actually become a drop target depends on how dnd-kit handles disabled sortables, which I couldn't confirm from this diff. Either way, the list-level logic and the comment disagree. Filter `sortableKeys` to the draggable keys and add a `canMove` guard in `handleDragEnd`, so the reorder logic doesn't rely on a side effect in another component.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="147-148"}

The drag handle is a `<button>` with `focus:outline-none` and no replacement focus style. The PR says keyboard reordering is supported (Tab to the handle, then Space and arrow keys), but a keyboard user gets no visible focus ring on the handle, so they can't tell which item is active.

The rest of the ui-v2 buttons use `focus-visible:ring-*`, which is the pattern to follow here.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-item.tsx" lines="84-92"}

dnd-kit's PointerSensor needs `touch-action: none` on the drag handle, or on touch devices the browser claims the gesture for scrolling and the drag never starts. The handle class list has `cursor-grab` but no `touch-none`.

This matters less than the critical bug, but it's a one-class fix.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-item.tsx" lines="84-92"}

The new snapshots include dnd-kit's generated ids, such as `DndDescribedBy-0`, `DndDescribedBy-1`, `DndLiveRegion-0`, and `aria-describedby="DndDescribedBy-2"`. These come from a module-level counter that increments on each DndContext mount. Their values depend on how many DndContexts earlier tests have already mounted, so adding, removing, or reordering a test in this file will break these snapshots even when the markup is unchanged.

The file header already warns that the snapshots depend on test order because of `useId`, so this adds to a known problem. It's worth knowing before the next test is added to the array block.

